---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ProjectHeader from '../../components/ProjectHeader.astro';
import Chart from '../../components/Chart.astro';
import NeuroPipeline from '../../components/NeuroPipeline.astro';

// Aggregate, cohort-level results only. No patient-level data is shown on this page.
const regimes = [
  { label: 'Zero-neurogenesis patients excluded', value: 29.08, slot: 2, note: 'Reference: the regression problem without the zero subgroup.' },
  { label: 'Gate + head trained on all patients', value: 472.9, slot: 1 },
  { label: 'No gate, head trained on all patients', value: 1145.73, slot: 'ref' },
  { label: 'Gate + head trained on non-zero only', value: 8795.35, slot: 'ref', note: 'Gate false negatives reach a regressor that never saw a zero.' },
  { label: 'No gate, head trained on non-zero only', value: 1e13, slot: 'ref', note: 'Approximate: unbounded extrapolation on zero patients.' },
];

// Leave-one-input-out ablation: change in patient-level relative error (percentage points) when the input is removed.
// Positive = removing the input hurts (the input helps).
const ablationRaw: [string, number][] = [
  ['EEG O2', 14.49], ['EEG F4', 12.35], ['Disease duration', 12.31], ['EEG Fz', 12.01], ['EEG Fp1', 10.43], ['EEG Cz', 10.12],
  ['EEG C4', 6.27], ['EEG F8', 5.35], ['EEG T5', 4.87], ['EEG T3', 3.04], ['EEG Pz', 2.86], ['EEG F3', 2.8], ['EEG P4', 2.46],
  ['EEG O1', 2.01], ['EEG F7', 1.9], ['Age', 1.66], ['EEG Fp2', 1.5], ['EEG T6', 1.24], ['EEG T4', -1.2], ['EEG P3', -1.22], ['EEG C3', -1.41],
];
const ablation = ablationRaw.map(([label, value]) => ({
  label, value,
  slot: label === 'Disease duration' ? 1 : label === 'Age' ? 2 : 'ref',
}));
---
<Base title="NeurogenesisGNN" description="Estimating patient-level hippocampal neurogenesis from EEG and clinical variables when thousands of clips come from only a few dozen patients.">
  <ProjectHeader
    title="NeurogenesisGNN"
    subtitle="Estimating hippocampal neurogenesis from EEG when thousands of clips come from only 30 patients."
    status="Ongoing"
    role="Part-time research assistant · owner of the code base and ML pipeline"
    period="Jun 2026 – present"
    team="Collaboration between the Bonaguidi Lab (USC) and USC InfoLab / IMSC"
    tags={['Small-n / high-d learning', 'Evaluation design', 'Biomedical ML', 'Interpretability']}
    tldr={[
      '<strong>13,000 clips ≠ 13,000 samples.</strong> The effective sample size is about 30 patients, so the prediction head is a statistical model (PLS + Ridge) on frozen EEG embeddings, not a deep network.',
      'Zero-neurogenesis patients behave like a <strong>separate regime</strong>. A gate trained on all patients cut error from ~1,146% to ~473%, but its misses remain the main failure mode.',
      'A <strong>learned percentile calibrator</strong> replaces the median for turning a patient’s clip predictions into one estimate.',
    ]}
  />

  <h2>The question</h2>
  <p>
    Hippocampal neurogenesis is reported to decline in epilepsy, but it can only be measured in brain tissue. Our collaborators in the
    Bonaguidi Lab asked whether it could be <em>estimated</em> from scalp EEG plus clinical variables such as age and disease duration.
    Alisha Cayce (Bonaguidi Lab) proposed using <strong>NeuroGNN</strong>, an EEG graph model developed at USC InfoLab (not my work),
    because it combines temporal, spatial and semantic structure. My job was to turn that idea into a working
    system: adapt NeuroGNN as a feature extractor and build the data pipeline, prediction head, evaluation and analysis around it.
  </p>

  <h2>The data regime decides the model</h2>
  <div class="stats">
    <div class="stat"><div class="num">30</div><div class="lbl">patients: the real number of independent samples</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="num">≈13k</div><div class="lbl">60-second EEG clips (correlated within patient)</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="num">≈6.4k</div><div class="lbl">embedding dimensions per clip</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="num">9</div><div class="lbl">patients with zero measured neurogenesis</div></div>
  </div>
  <p>
    I noticed the mismatch on day one. Clips from the same patient are not independent, so ~13k clips do not give a deep prediction head
    enough signal, and a ~6.4k-dimensional input with this few subjects overfits easily. I compared Lasso, Ridge, SVR, PLS and
    <strong>PLS + Ridge</strong>, and the combination worked best. PLS finds a few directions in the embedding that covary with the label
    (supervised dimensionality reduction). Ridge then shrinks the weights on those components. Every step, scaling included, is fitted on
    training folds only.
  </p>

  <NeuroPipeline />

  <h2>Evaluating at the right unit</h2>
  <p>
    Splitting clips at random is fast and gives stable numbers, but clips from one patient land in both train and test, so it does not measure
    generalization to <em>new patients</em>. Early on, patient-level folds were too unstable to guide development: some folds held a single
    patient with a few minutes of EEG. I therefore used clip-level CV only as a development signal and treated it as such. As the cohort and
    recordings grow, patient-level evaluation becomes the yardstick for any final claim.
  </p>
  <p>
    Turning a patient’s clip predictions into one number also matters. The clip predictions have heavy outliers, so I first used the
    <strong>median</strong>. Looking at individual patients showed the median was often not the closest percentile. The
    <strong>calibrator</strong> learns from training patients which percentile of the clip-prediction distribution usually lands nearest
    the true label, then reads that percentile off each test patient’s distribution.
  </p>

  <h2>A second regime: zero neurogenesis</h2>
  <p>
    Plotting prediction quality patient by patient showed that most severe outliers were patients with <em>zero</em> measured neurogenesis.
    Two problems compound here. The relative-error metric is close to undefined near zero, since a 1-unit miss becomes a huge percentage.
    A linear head also cannot fit a qualitative “off” state that sits far below every non-zero patient. A logistic zero/non-zero gate
    (hurdle model) in front of the regressor helps, but which data each part sees matters a great deal:
  </p>
  <Chart
    title="Patient-level relative error by configuration (log scale)"
    spec={{ type: 'hbar', items: regimes, log: true, unit: '%', decimals: 1, xLabel: 'relative error (%, log scale)' }}
    caption="Without the zero subgroup, the same pipeline reaches ~29%. With zero patients included, the best setup is a gate plus a regressor that has also seen zeros, so it degrades gracefully when the gate misses. A non-zero-only regressor without a gate extrapolates without bound. The zero subgroup currently looks like a different, likely non-linear regime, which is a hypothesis rather than a finding."
    table={{ head: ['Configuration', 'Relative error (%)'], rows: regimes.map((r) => [r.label, r.value >= 1e6 ? '≈1e13' : r.value.toFixed(2)]) }}
  />

  <h2>What the inputs contribute</h2>
  <p>
    A leave-one-input-out ablation, alongside SHAP analysis, measures how much patient-level error rises when each input is removed. Two results
    were instructive:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Disease duration</strong> is among the most useful inputs, consistent with clinical reports that neurogenesis declines with longer epilepsy duration.</li>
    <li><strong>Age</strong> adds little. The age–neurogenesis literature is mostly about healthy adults, and in this cohort the disease effect seems to dominate.</li>
    <li>Inputs whose removal <em>helps</em> only help slightly. PLS assigns little weight to directions unrelated to the label, and Ridge limits what is left.</li>
  </ul>
  <Chart
    title="Increase in patient-level error when one input is removed (percentage points)"
    spec={{ type: 'hbar', items: ablation, signed: true, decimals: 1, xLabel: 'Δ relative error when removed (pp) · higher = more useful input' }}
    caption="Baseline (all inputs, zero patients excluded): 29.1% relative error. These are predictive contributions in this cohort, not causal medical conclusions."
    table={{ head: ['Removed input', 'Δ relative error (pp)'], rows: ablationRaw.map(([k, v]) => [k, v.toFixed(2)]) }}
  />

  <h2>What this project taught me</h2>
  <p>
    How to work with data that are scarce, heterogeneous and unevenly distributed. That means choosing models by effective sample size,
    being explicit about what each evaluation can show, and finding subgroups that break a model. It was also my first time adapting
    someone else’s model as a component for a new objective.
  </p>

  <h2>Next</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Final results on patient-level cross-validation as the cohort grows toward the planned size.</li>
    <li>Separate learned models for the zero and non-zero regimes behind the hurdle gate, and better calibrated gating.</li>
    <li>Per-patient prediction variance as a first-class output, a prerequisite for anything clinical.</li>
    <li>Add MRI as a further modality; explore fine-tuning the encoder on this task.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
